# Rust
/target/
**/*.rs.bk

# Cargo.lock is deliberately NOT ignored: Armada ships binaries, and a
# committed lockfile is what makes a release build reproducible.

# Node
node_modules/
dist/
out/
.vite/

# macOS
.DS_Store

# Editors
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# Agent scratch. Both name whatever a session was about, which has no business
# in a public repository.
.gnhf/
.lavish/

# Golden-comparison output, written beside a fixture when one fails so the diff
# can be read. Never the fixture itself.
*.actual

# Storybook builds from source on demand; the output is not a record of anything.
storybook-static/

# What a Fleet pointed at this repository writes into it. `.armada/workflows/`
# is this repository declaring its own workflows and is checked in; everything
# else under `.armada/` is a record of one run on one machine — a Job's log, its
# Checks' output, its worktree, its Drones' transcripts, the files attached to
# its brief, and `.armada/<job-id>/`, where a Drone puts anything it writes for
# itself.
#
# Listed as an allowlist rather than one line per directory. The list form left
# `.armada/worktrees/` untracked but unignored for a while, and a path keyed by
# a Job id cannot be named in advance at all.
#
# **A Drone's scratch is ignored here, and that is this repository's call
# rather than Armada's.** A repository that wants a Drone's working files in
# history deletes the first line and gets them, keyed by Job id and colliding
# with nothing. This one is public and the workspace those files would quote is
# not — the same reason `.gnhf/` and `.lavish/` are above. The cost is that
# scratch is reclaimed with the worktree it sits in, so it lives as long as the
# Job does and no longer.
#
# **`.armada/artifacts/` is not scratch and is not ignored.** It holds what a
# step was asked to deliver — the file its `artifact_exists` check names, which
# the next step reads and the Judge is shown. It has to be visible for that to
# be true: Fleet reads the branch diff with untracked files included and
# ignored ones excluded, so a deliverable under an ignored path is a
# deliverable no Judge can open. That was measured on a real Job, which spent
# nineteen hours being refused for a root cause written on page one of a file
# nothing could see.
.armada/*
!.armada/workflows/
!.armada/artifacts/
